---
import BaseLayout from '../../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../../../components/navigation/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { buildLocalePath } from '../../../../i18n/utils';

const locale = 'en-US' as const;

const title = 'How Much House Can You Afford on Your Salary? (2026 Guide)';

const description =
  'Work out how much house you can afford from your salary with the 28/36 rule: turn your pay into a maximum monthly payment, then see what that buys at a 7.28% rate with 20% down.';

const canonical = '/en-us/blog/how-much-house-can-you-afford-2026/';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BlogPosting',
  headline: title,
  description,
  datePublished: '2026-11-09',
  dateModified: '2026-11-09',
  author: {
    '@type': 'Organization',
    name: 'PayRenTax',
    url: 'https://payrentax.me',
  },
  publisher: {
    '@type': 'Organization',
    name: 'PayRenTax',
    url: 'https://payrentax.me',
  },
  mainEntityOfPage: {
    '@type': 'WebPage',
    '@id': 'https://payrentax.me/en-us/blog/how-much-house-can-you-afford-2026/',
  },
};

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'How much house can I afford on a $60,000 salary?',
    a: 'Using the 28% rule, a $60,000 salary gives a gross monthly income of $5,000, so the maximum monthly housing payment is $5,000 × 0.28 = $1,400. That $1,400 has to cover principal, interest, property tax, and insurance together.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the 28/36 rule?',
    a: 'The 28/36 rule is the guideline lenders use: your total monthly housing payment should not exceed 28% of your gross monthly income, and your total monthly debts including housing should not exceed 36% of your gross monthly income.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What does PITI stand for?',
    a: 'PITI stands for principal, interest, property tax, and insurance — the four parts of a monthly mortgage payment. The 28% rule applies to the whole PITI amount, not just principal and interest.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How much is a 20% down payment on a $250,000 house?',
    a: 'A 20% down payment on a $250,000 house is $250,000 × 0.20 = $50,000, leaving a loan of $200,000. On a $200,000 house the same 20% down payment is $40,000, and on a $300,000 house it is $60,000.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does the state I live in change how much house I can afford?',
    a: 'Yes. Property tax rates on the PayRenTax calculator page range from 0.29% in Hawaii to 1.88% in New Jersey and Illinois, so the same house can cost about $265 a month more in tax in a high-tax state than in a low-tax state — and that tax comes out of the same 28% budget.',
  },
];

---

<BaseLayout locale={locale} title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical}>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />
    <script
      type="application/ld+json"
      set:html={JSON.stringify({
        '@context': 'https://schema.org',
        '@type': 'FAQPage',
        mainEntity: faqItems.map((f) => ({
          '@type': 'Question',
          name: f.q,
          acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
        })),
      })}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <main class="page">
    <Breadcrumbs locale={locale} trail={[{ label: 'Blog', href: buildLocalePath(locale, 'blog') }, { label: 'How Much House Can You Afford', href: buildLocalePath(locale, 'blog/how-much-house-can-you-afford-2026') }]} />
    <article class="article">
      <header class="article-header">
        <p class="article-date">November 9, 2026</p>
        <h1>{title}</h1>
        <p class="article-intro">{description}</p>
      </header>

      <p>
        A salary tells you what you earn, but it does not directly tell you
        what house you can buy. The link between the two is your monthly
        housing payment: lenders compare it with your monthly income, and the
        comparison they use is the 28/36 rule. This guide shows how to turn
        any salary into a maximum monthly payment, what goes into that
        payment, and what it buys — using only the figures on our house
        affordability calculator page and simple arithmetic.
      </p>

      <div class="tldr-box">
        <p><strong>TL;DR:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Max monthly housing payment</strong> = gross monthly
            income × 28%.
          </li>
          <li>
            On a <strong>$60,000 salary</strong>, that is $5,000 × 0.28 =
            <strong>$1,400 a month</strong>.
          </li>
          <li>
            The payment is <strong>PITI</strong>: principal, interest,
            property tax, and insurance — all four together.
          </li>
          <li>
            A <strong>20% down payment</strong> means the loan is 80% of the
            home price: $40,000 down on a $200,000 house.
          </li>
          <li>
            Your <strong>state changes the answer</strong>: property tax
            runs from 0.29% to 1.88%, a difference of about $265 a month on
            a $200,000 house.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
        <p><strong>On this page</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="#what-it-means">What "How Much House Can I Afford" Means</a></li>
          <li><a href="#rule">The 28% Rule in 3 Steps</a></li>
          <li><a href="#piti">What Goes Into Your Monthly Payment</a></li>
          <li><a href="#example">Worked Example: A $200,000 Home on a $60,000 Salary</a></li>
          <li><a href="#state">Why Your State Changes the Answer</a></li>
          <li><a href="#faq">Frequently Asked Questions</a></li>
        </ul>
      </nav>

      <section id="what-it-means" class="article-section">
        <h2>What "How Much House Can I Afford" Means</h2>
        <p>
          "How much house can I afford" is really a question about a monthly
          payment, not a house price. A lender does not start from the price
          of a house; it starts from your income and works out the largest
          monthly payment that income can support. The house price follows
          from that payment, the interest rate, the down payment, and the
          property tax where you live.
        </p>
        <p>
          The guideline lenders use is the <strong>28/36 rule</strong>, which
          is stated on our
          <a href="/en-us/salary/house-affordability/">house affordability
          calculator</a> page. It has two parts. The front-end ratio says
          your total monthly housing payment should not exceed 28% of your
          gross monthly income. The back-end ratio says your total monthly
          debts — housing plus everything else, such as car loans and
          student loans — should not exceed 36% of your gross monthly
          income. Both ratios use gross income, before deductions, so start
          from the salary figure itself, not your take-home pay. Our guide
          on
          <a href="/en-us/blog/take-home-pay-explained-2026/">take-home
          pay</a> explains the difference between the two.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="rule" class="article-section">
        <h2>The 28% Rule in 3 Steps</h2>
        <p>
          Finding your maximum monthly housing payment takes three steps,
          and you only need your annual salary:
        </p>

        <div class="formula-box">
          <p><strong>Gross monthly income = annual salary ÷ 12</strong></p>
          <p><strong>Max monthly housing payment = gross monthly income × 0.28</strong></p>
        </div>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>Divide your salary by 12.</strong> That is your gross
            monthly income.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Multiply by 0.28.</strong> That is the largest monthly
            housing payment under the 28% rule.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Multiply by 0.36 for the back-end check.</strong> That is
            the largest your total monthly debts, housing included, should
            be.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <div class="example-box">
          <p><strong>Example: $60,000 salary</strong></p>
          <ul>
            <li>Gross monthly income: $60,000 ÷ 12 = <strong>$5,000</strong></li>
            <li>Max housing payment: $5,000 × 0.28 = <strong>$1,400 per month</strong></li>
            <li>Max total debts: $5,000 × 0.36 = <strong>$1,800 per month</strong></li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Annual salary</th>
              <th>Gross monthly income</th>
              <th>Max housing payment (28%)</th>
              <th>Max total debts (36%)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>$50,000</td>
              <td>$4,166.67</td>
              <td>$1,166.67</td>
              <td>$1,500.00</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>$60,000</td>
              <td>$5,000.00</td>
              <td>$1,400.00</td>
              <td>$1,800.00</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>$75,000</td>
              <td>$6,250.00</td>
              <td>$1,750.00</td>
              <td>$2,250.00</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>$80,000</td>
              <td>$6,666.67</td>
              <td>$1,866.67</td>
              <td>$2,400.00</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>$100,000</td>
              <td>$8,333.33</td>
              <td>$2,333.33</td>
              <td>$3,000.00</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>$125,000</td>
              <td>$10,416.67</td>
              <td>$2,916.67</td>
              <td>$3,750.00</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>$150,000</td>
              <td>$12,500.00</td>
              <td>$3,500.00</td>
              <td>$4,500.00</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>
          The back-end figure matters as much as the front-end one. On a
          $60,000 salary, the 36% limit is $1,800 a month for all debts
          combined. If your housing payment is $1,400, that leaves $400 a
          month for every other debt payment. If your other debts are larger
          than that, the house you can afford is smaller than the 28% figure
          alone suggests. You can check the salary behind any of these
          figures with our
          <a href="/en-us/salary/annual-salary-calculator/">annual salary
          calculator</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="piti" class="article-section">
        <h2>What Goes Into Your Monthly Payment</h2>
        <p>
          The monthly payment the 28% rule limits is PITI: principal,
          interest, property tax, and insurance. All four parts come out of
          the same budget, so a high property tax leaves less room for
          principal and interest — and therefore buys a cheaper house.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Principal and interest.</strong> This is the loan itself.
            Our calculator page uses a 7.28% 30-year fixed rate, from the
            Freddie Mac Primary Mortgage Market Survey for the week ending
            October 1, 2026. At that rate, every $160,000 borrowed costs
            $1,094.74 a month in principal and interest.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Property tax.</strong> The calculator page uses each
            state's effective rate on owner-occupied housing, from 0.29% in
            Hawaii to 1.88% in New Jersey and Illinois. On a $200,000 house,
            that is $48.33 a month at 0.29% and $313.33 a month at 1.88%.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Homeowner's insurance.</strong> The calculator page uses
            0.20% of the home value per year. On a $200,000 house, that is
            $400 a year, or $33.33 a month.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Down payment.</strong> The calculator page assumes 20%
            down, which also means no private mortgage insurance. A 20%
            down payment is $40,000 on a $200,000 house, $50,000 on a
            $250,000 house, and $60,000 on a $300,000 house — and the loan
            is the remaining 80%.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          To see your own numbers instantly, run your salary and state
          through our
          <a href="/en-us/salary/house-affordability/">house affordability
          calculator</a>, which applies the same 28% rule, rate, down
          payment, and state tax figures.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="example" class="article-section">
        <h2>Worked Example: A $200,000 Home on a $60,000 Salary</h2>
        <p>
          Take a $60,000 salary and a $200,000 house, using the calculator
          page's figures: 20% down, a 7.28% 30-year fixed rate, and the
          lowest property tax rate of 0.29%. The steps are:
        </p>

        <div class="example-box">
          <p><strong>Example: $60,000 salary, $200,000 house, 0.29% property tax</strong></p>
          <ul>
            <li>Down payment: $200,000 × 0.20 = <strong>$40,000</strong></li>
            <li>Loan: $200,000 − $40,000 = <strong>$160,000</strong></li>
            <li>Principal and interest: <strong>$1,094.74 per month</strong></li>
            <li>Property tax: $200,000 × 0.0029 ÷ 12 = <strong>$48.33 per month</strong></li>
            <li>Insurance: $200,000 × 0.0020 ÷ 12 = <strong>$33.33 per month</strong></li>
            <li>Total PITI: $1,094.74 + $48.33 + $33.33 = <strong>$1,176.40 per month</strong></li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <p>
          The 28% limit on a $60,000 salary is $1,400 a month, so a $1,176.40
          payment fits — with $223.60 a month of room to spare. The 36%
          back-end limit is $1,800, so after this housing payment there is
          $623.60 a month left for all other debt payments combined. If your
          other debts are within that, the house fits both parts of the
          rule. To check what the same salary means per paycheck, run it
          through our
          <a href="/en-us/salary/paycheck-calculator/">paycheck
          calculator</a> — our guide on
          <a href="/en-us/blog/how-to-calculate-your-paycheck-2026/">how to
          calculate your paycheck</a> explains the steps.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="state" class="article-section">
        <h2>Why Your State Changes the Answer</h2>
        <p>
          The same salary and the same house price do not buy the same house
          everywhere, because property tax is part of the monthly payment.
          On the $200,000 house above, the tax alone is $48.33 a month at
          Hawaii's 0.29% rate and $313.33 a month at New Jersey's 1.88%
          rate — a difference of $265.00 a month for the same house:
        </p>

        <div class="example-box">
          <p><strong>Same $200,000 house, same $60,000 salary</strong></p>
          <ul>
            <li>At 0.29% property tax: $1,094.74 + $48.33 + $33.33 = <strong>$1,176.40 per month — fits the $1,400 limit</strong></li>
            <li>At 1.88% property tax: $1,094.74 + $313.33 + $33.33 = <strong>$1,441.40 per month — $41.40 over the $1,400 limit</strong></li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <p>
          In the high-tax case, the payment is $41.40 over the limit, so the
          buyer would need a cheaper house, a larger down payment, or a
          higher salary to make the same $200,000 house fit. That is why the
          calculator asks for your state first: the tax rate is set before
          any of the other math begins. If you are weighing renting against
          buying, our guide on
          <a href="/en-us/blog/how-much-rent-can-i-afford-2026/">how much
          rent you can afford</a> applies the same kind of income-based
          budget to rent instead.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="faq" class="article-section">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        {faqItems.map((item) => (
          <>
            <h3>{item.q}</h3>
            <p>{item.a}</p>
          </>
        ))}
      </section>

      <section id="related" class="article-section">
        <h2>Related Articles</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="/en-us/blog/how-much-rent-can-i-afford-2026/">How Much Rent Can I Afford in 2026?</a></li>
          <li><a href="/en-us/blog/what-is-an-annual-salary-2026/">What Is an Annual Salary? (2026)</a></li>
          <li><a href="/en-us/blog/how-to-calculate-your-paycheck-2026/">How to Calculate Your Paycheck (2026)</a></li>
          <li><a href="/en-us/blog/take-home-pay-explained-2026/">Take-Home Pay Explained (2026)</a></li>
          <li><a href="/en-us/blog/salary-to-hourly-conversion-2026/">Salary to Hourly Conversion (2026)</a></li>
        </ul>
      </section>

    </article>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
